Z||000018||84Bill||04-28-2001||12:03 AM||||Hummm I dont understand, you have the car up and the wheels off? pull the rear of the pipe out and kinda wriggle it around to get the front out your gonna need a bit of space to do it. Passanger side is the easiest to do. I only did the P side and it took 15 minutes. The drivers side is a bit more challanging but should not be giving you this much grief  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000019||sqoach||04-28-2001||12:23 AM||||The wheel isn't off, but it's not even in the way (yet). The brakes lines coming from the master cylinder are wrapped around the coolant pipe.<P>There's nothing that I can see in the way from the passenger side coolant pipe. I could see how that wouldn't take long at all.||||reg||1
Z||000020||84Bill||04-28-2001||12:43 AM||||Tell ya what I need to put some new antifreeze in my unit so I'm gonna go pull the D/S pipe out and I'll tell ya how when I get it done.<P>You got mail<P>Looks like the pipe is hanging up on the spare tire well, I losened the brake line and there is plenty of room for the pipe around it but its bumping against the well. I'll get a better look at it tommorow. I removed the front tire and removed the bottom bolt from the shock and I also removed the two bolts the hold the brakeline in place. the biggest problem I've had so far is the hose clamp in the front sent almost the whole hour (not a lot of room up there) twisting and pulling it b4 it finally broke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000021||Tweek||04-28-2001||12:06 PM||||Hey Sqoach, Last year I had an 87 Grand am that was having major overheating probs. It was a duke. not a quad4. I was told that I should be able to take the cap off and watch the coolant cycling through... Well everytime I did that It just boiled right out in a few seconds, just like a mini volcano. Turned out there was a crack in the head. They charged me $400 to fix it, including the cost of a new head. I hope this isnt your problem, but cracked heads tend to be commen in dukes I think...  good luck! i know that overheating can be one of the most frustrating things to fix...||||reg||1
Z||000022||Seanh||04-28-2001||02:13 PM||||With all the work involved (my experience is with the V6 only) IMO $400 parts & Labor is pretty reasonable to fix a craked head. I think the 4 cyl. engine should be a heck of alot easier then the V6, and I thought the V6 was fairly easy, just time consuming.||||reg||1
